/*
    On booleans, `&&&` is MUTUALLY guarded conjunction: each operand guards the other.
    The result is true when both are true, false when either is false, and undefined only
    when neither can be settled - regardless of which operand is examined first.
    Where `&&` picks a direction, `&&&` is symmetric, which is what makes it the right
    choice when either side may be the one that is cheap or defined.
*/

let n = 0 in

n != 0 &&& 100 / n > 1
